  "text": "The study examined whether heating the larger body in a gravity experiment changes the measured attraction. Conventional calculations of planetary masses assume no temperature influence, but direct tests are scarce because earlier experiments only warmed the smaller body up to about 100 °C, leaving the question unresolved. An earlier set of careful measurements with a torsion‑balance device suggested a small positive temperature coefficient for the gravitational constant, on the order of 10⁻⁵ per degree. In the present work the apparatus was upgraded: the support structure was replaced by a more rigid metal frame, and improved thermal shielding was added between the heated large masses and the vacuum chamber holding the small masses. Across eleven measurement series, the average change in deflection between hot and cold conditions was –0.05 mm on a 208 mm scale, roughly 0.025 % over a 200 °C range. Most runs showed a negative shift, with two showing a slight increase; the scatter is comparable to the observed effect. Consequently, any temperature dependence of gravity, if present, is smaller than about 2 × 10⁻⁶ per degree and may be zero. The earlier larger effect is likely explained by minute, reversible shifts in the relative positions of the masses as temperature varied."
